statement:
E.1a (Proved: saturation as a precise polarity).. Let $G/\mathbb Q$ be a reductive algebraic group, with its category of finite-dimensional rational representations. Let $\mathcal I$ be the set of complex cocharacters, modulo conjugation if desired. For $C\subset\mathcal I$ and a class $\mathcal E$ of representations, define \[  egin{split} E(C)&=\{V:	ext{every weight of every }
u\in C 	ext{ on }V_{\mathbb C}	ext{ is nonnegative}\},\ I(\mathcal E)&=\{
u:	ext{every weight of }
u 	ext{ on every }V\in\mathcal E 	ext{ is nonnegative}\},\ \operatorname{Sat}(C)&=I(E(C)). \end{split} \] Then $\operatorname{Sat}$ is extensive, monotone, and idempotent, and $E(\operatorname{Sat}(C))=E(C)$. The exact missing condition is  egin{equation} C=I(E(C)). \end{equation} The saturated cocharacter sets are in inclusion-reversing bijection with the full representation subcategories that are intersections of the $P(
u)$. Additional positivity, Tate-twist, weight-zero, and Galois-invariance axioms are imposed on the objects on both sides; they do not replace \eqref{eq:effective-saturation}.

